---
run: round-trip
fixpoint: executed
---
-- book.sh --
#!/bin/sh
# floor30-atlas-dot-with-operands — QUESTION: given `. ./seer.sh x y`, does the sourced file see `x`
# as its own `$1`, or the caller's `$1`? And are the caller's positionals restored afterwards?
#
# Why the inclusion design needs it: operands on `.` are an XSI/common extension, NOT base POSIX, so
# this is exactly the shape `two-binary-floor` exists to adjudicate.
#
# ANSWERED — and the answer is a DISAGREEMENT, which gate-9 has nowhere to commit: an
# `expected.emitted` is by construction what the binaries AGREED on. So the measurement lives here,
# in prose, and this case carries NO `expected.emitted` section — it does not opt into the
# differential, and `mise run test:floor` stays green while the answer stays durable. RE-ARMING is
# one line: add an empty `-- expected.emitted --` section back and the lane re-measures (and goes
# red again, which is the trade this shape makes).
#
# measured-divergence: (mise run test:floor -- floor30-atlas, dash 0.5.12 ∩ posh 0.14.1, WSL)
#   dash: file-entry: 1=[caller1] 2=[caller2] count=[2]
#         book-after: 1=[caller1] 2=[caller2] count=[2]
#   posh: file-entry: 1=[x] 2=[y] count=[3]
#         book-after: 1=[caller1] 2=[caller2] count=[2]
#   MEANING: dash IGNORES the operands outright — the sourced file simply keeps reading the caller's
#   own positionals — while posh HONOURS them and restores the caller's afterwards. The two do not
#   merely differ in a status; they give one authored line two different meanings, so `. file args`
#   is OUTSIDE the base dialect. No inclusion lowering may emit it, and a book that writes it means
#   different things on different hosts, which is a correctness question and not a portability
#   nicety.
#   SURPRISE this case cannot explain: posh reports `count=[3]` for TWO operands, so a third
#   positional exists that nothing here names. Whatever it is, it is one more reason the shape is
#   not dialect material.
set -- caller1 caller2
. ./seer.sh x y
printf 'book-after: 1=[%s] 2=[%s] count=[%s]\n' "$1" "$2" "$#"

-- seer.sh --
printf 'file-entry: 1=[%s] 2=[%s] count=[%s]\n' "$1" "$2" "$#"

-- expected.ran --

-- replay --
$ dorc --book=book.sh --results -
#!/bin/sh
# dorc probe (read-only): checks per-SITE convergence, mutates nothing.
# When run, emits one results-record per site on stdout (the return channel):
#   site <leafid> effect=<holds|absent|cant-tell> rc=<n>
# effect is derived from the probe command's rc (0=holds, 1=absent, else cant-tell);
# rc is the raw PROBE-command status (opaque to Dorc -- the record is the out-of-band lane).

printf 'dorc-records/1 nonce=dorc attempt=1 host=localhost book=e0dcf1c0771a1d61d82e0dfa819ef2f8e499274a315ddab519001bf80b129cce sites=0 @@dorc@@\n'
# site:0 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:1 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:2 unresolvable-no-probe
printf 'dorc-records-end/1 nonce=dorc @@dorc@@\n'
#!/bin/sh
# dorc apply: the book, with already-converged/dead lines elided (value-preserving stand-in).

